        "date": "1845-05-06",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "6 May 1845",
        "text": "It is logical indeed--and rational, ... which logic is not always ... and if I had 'the tongue of men and of angels,' I would use it to persuade you.",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s1810",
        "messageOrdinal": 124,
        "sender": "Elizabeth",
        "date": "1845-10-11",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "11 October 1845",
        "text": "And what was the use of telling Mr. Kenyon that you were 'quite well' when you know you are not?",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s3158",
        "messageOrdinal": 185,
        "sender": "Elizabeth",
        "date": "1845-12-30",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "30 December 1845",
        "text": "The Italians would say it better without saying more.",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s3718",
        "messageOrdinal": 205,
        "sender": "Elizabeth",
        "date": "1846-01-19",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "19 January 1846",
        "text": "A hundred letters I have, by this last, ... to set against Napoleon's Hundred Days--did you know that?",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s3867",
        "messageOrdinal": 212,
        "sender": "Robert",
        "date": "1846-01-26",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "26 January 1846",
        "text": "You have said thus to me--in the very last letter, indeed.",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s5868",
        "messageOrdinal": 303,
        "sender": "Elizabeth",
        "date": "1846-04-08",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "8 April 1846",
        "text": "Landor will be gratified and grateful ... he, allowably—and only you shall be ‘hateful’ ... and only to me, dearest, ... so that it doesn’t matter much.",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s9040",
        "messageOrdinal": 440,
        "sender": "Robert",
        "date": "1846-07-06",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "6 July 1846",
        "text": "Mrs. Jameson showed me the lines you had sent her, Horne’s very beautiful poem,—very earnest, very solemn and pathetic,—worthy of Horne and the subject—and you will do well to reward him as you propose.",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s9902",
        "messageOrdinal": 476,
        "sender": "Elizabeth",
        "date": "1846-07-29",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "29 July 1846",
        "text": "It appears that he talked to my sisters some time before he let himself be announced to me—he said to them ‘I want to talk to you ... sit down by me and listen.’",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      }
    ]
  }
